rump drivers in userspace
=========================

This is an extension of Robert Millan's work with rump and Hurd.
Thanks to luckyluke for blockrump.c


hurd/libmachdevrump/

This folder contains an implementation of the mach device RPC
and presents a character device to the system as a device node,
using a rumpkernel to provide access to raw disk storage via pci in
userspace.  (It is mostly a copy of libmachdev with the block device
implementation from linux replaced with a rump one).

WIP: Currently requires the hurd repository as a dependency,
     since it is built as part of hurd.

Status: Mounts disk after 20 seconds (still has irq problems)


hurd/rumpdisk/

This folder contains the translator main function that calls
into libmachdevrump.


rump-patches/

This folder has a few patches needed for rump


Preparation:
Drop libmachdevrump/ and rumpdisk/ into the root of a recent hurd checkout.
Add to the main hurd Makefile the lib-dirs = ... libmachdevrump

Build and install rump:
	
	git clone https://github.com/rumpkernel/buildrump.sh.git
	cd buildrump.sh
	#(apply 0001-buildrump.patch)
	./buildrump.sh checkout
	cd src
	#(apply 0001-buildrump-src.patch)
	cd ..
	./buildrump.sh fullbuild
	#(make coffee)
	cd ..
	git clone https://github.com/zamaudio/pci-userspace.git
	cd pci-userspace
	git checkout upstreaming-rump-hurd
	cd src-gnu
	# Don't use the submodule buildrump.sh that comes with pci-userspace
	../../buildrump.sh/obj/tooldir/rumpmake dependall

	Find all the libs librump*.so that buildrump.sh created and install 
	them somewhere in /lib/i386-gnu or /usr/lib
	You probably also need the headers installed to /usr/include/rump
	Look in rump/ for outputs
	
	The rumpmake step is awkward because the libs are compiled in the src
	directory, so I provided a list of files to grab from the tree in
	"rump-extra-libs".

Build the libmachdevrump component of hurd

Build rumpdisk as a hurd utility (its the translator)


Running:
	WARNING: This will probably clash with your system's IDE /dev/hd0s3

	sudo touch /dev/rumpdisk
	sudo touch /dev/wd0
	sudo settrans /dev/wd0 /hurd/devnode -M /dev/rumpdisk wd0
	sudo settrans /dev/rumpdisk /hurd/rumpdisk
	sudo settrans -fga /mnt /hurd/ext2fs -T typed part:3:device:/dev/wd0

	This works on my system but takes about 20 seconds to mount due
	to bad interrupts.


test/

This is a simplified test case that attempts to open a device node internal
to rump kernel with rump_sys_open() to test whether the pci device can work.

As root:
	make
	RUMP_VERBOSE=yes ./open-rump /dev/wd0d

